@meta
id: 20-02
title: Drill — yank, delete, "0p
chapter: 20
type: drill
skills: register-zero
par: 16
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: copy, cut, paste, get the wrong thing, sigh
open: orders.csv

@brief
Eight times: the third row is a mangled copy of the second. Yank the
good row, delete the bad one, and put a fresh copy of the good row at
the bottom of the file. Yank first, delete second, put last, every rep.

By rep four `"0p` should come out of your fingers whenever there has
been a delete since the yank.

@keys
| `yy` | yank the line |
| `dd` | delete the line — and overwrite the unnamed register |
| `"0p` | put the last yank, ignoring the delete |

@hints
`j` `yy` takes the good row. `j` `dd` removes the bad one and leaves you
on the last row.
--
Plain `p` here would put the mangled row back. `"0p` puts the yank.

@solution
    j yy j dd "0p :wq<Enter>

Thirteen keystrokes, and the only one that is new is the `"0` in front
of the put. The habit you are building is a question you ask on the way
to `p`: has anything been deleted since I yanked? If yes, `"0p`. In a
week you will stop hearing the question and just press the right one.

@start orders.csv
id,customer,status,total
ord_001,cus_042,PENDING,1999
ord_00,cus_042,PENDNG,1999
ord_002,cus_017,SHIPPED,4500

@expected orders.csv
id,customer,status,total
ord_001,cus_042,PENDING,1999
ord_002,cus_017,SHIPPED,4500
ord_001,cus_042,PENDING,1999
